    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> at Hadrian's wish in 138, Marcus was trained for rule for over twenty years and on his accession in 161 insisted on sharing power with his adoptive brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a>.</li><li>His reign was taken up by war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> in the east and with the Marcomanni, Quadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, while the Antonine Plague killed five to ten million people.</li><li>The Meditations, written in Greek on campaign for his own guidance, made him the model of the philosopher king, though historians have criticised his choice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a> as successor.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The bronze equestrian statue of Marcus Aurelius, made in c. 175 and now in the Capitoline Museums, is the only Roman equestrian statue to have survived into the modern period.</p></aside>
    </section>

